Dogen was a Zen priest in the early Kamakura period.
He was the founder of the Soto sect.
In his last years, he was also called Maregen.
In the same sect, he is also referred to as the founder of the sect.
His shigo (a posthumous name) was Busshodenshi and Shoyo Daishi (a priest who has been given the title of priest).
It is generally called Dogen Zenji.
It is said to have spread the method of making and cleaning in Japan and cleaning the custom of eating.
There is a theory that he first brought back Momoo-chiku (Moxian) to Japan.
There are many theories about Dogen's birth, but there is a theory that he was born in the direct line of Naidaijin (minister of the center) Michichika TSUCHIMIKADO (MINAMOTO no Michichika or Michichika KOGA).
According to the standard theory, he was born as the son of Michichika and FUJIWARA no Koreko, the daughter of Daijo-daijin (Grand minister of state) Motofusa MATSUDONO (FUJIWARA no Motofusa) in Matsudono villa in Kyoto, but recently, the theory that he was the real child of Michitomo HORIKAWA's adoptive father is widely accepted.
There is also a theory that Michichika's son, MINAMOTO no Michimune or Michimitsu KOGA, was Michichika's father.
According to the biography "Kenchoki," he lost his father (Michichika) at the age of three and was adopted by his older paternal half-brother, Michitomo HORIKAWA.
According to one theory, after the death of his maternal uncle, Moroie MATSUDONO (the former Naidaijin (the minister of the center)), he wanted to have an heir of the Matsudono family, but it is said that Dogen, who felt unprecedented life.
There is a theory that Shinran, the founder of Jodo Shinshu (the True Pure Land Sect of Buddhism), was a relative of each other, but there is no evidence that he had a deep knowledge on his mother's side.
There is a theory that the chapter of 'Shobogenzo' in his book "Shobogenzo" was written by Shinran.
In 1213, he visited Ryoken, a maternal uncle of Mt. Hiei.
In 1214, he became a priest of Tendai-zasu (head priest of the Tendai sect) Kinen, and named himself Hobo Dogen.
In 1215, he studied Tendai Sect under Kintane of Onjo-ji Temple.
In 1217, he studied under Myozen, a disciple of Eisai at Kennin-ji Temple.
In 1223, along with Myozen, he traveled to the Southern Sung Dynasty to the Southern Sung Dynasty, and received Inka from Joko of the Soto sect.
In 1228, he returned to Japan.
In 1233, he founded Kosho-ji Temple in Fukakusa, Kyoto.
Around this time, he was oppressed from Mt. Hiei.
In July 1243, he was invited by Yoshishige HATANO, jito (manager and lord of manor) of Echizen Province, and moved to Shibisho, Echizen Province.
On the way, he responded to the invitation of Nobutsuna SASAKI, a feudal lord of Kutsuki Village, and stayed at the Kutsuki-mura Village (Takashima City) (the origin of Kosho-ji Temple (Takashima City).
In 1244, he founded the Daibutsu-ji Temple in Kasamatsu.
In 1246, he changed his name to Eihei-ji Temple and changed his name to Maregen.
Around this time, he went down to Kamakura for the invitation of the regent Tokiyori HOJO and Yoshishige HATANO.
It was a half year of the introduction of Buddhism in Kamakura, but it became the beginning of the purity of pure Zen Buddhism in the Kanto region.
In 1253, he transferred Eihei-ji Temple to his disciple Enojo, and died at the residence of his disciple Kakunen (Takaji-dori Nishinotoin-dori Street, Kyoto City) at the age of 54.
The cause of his death is said to be ulberculosis.
