Umekoji Steam Locomotive Museum is a facility for the preservation of steam locomotives operated by West Japan Railway Company (JR West) in Kanzaki-cho, Shimogyo Ward, Kyoto City, Kyoto Prefecture.
On October 10, 1972, the Japan Railway Company was established to commemorate the 100th anniversary of the establishment of Japan Railway Company (JR West) in Shimogyo Ward, Kyoto City, and it was positioned as a valuable industrial cultural property for the first time to support Japanese railway transportation in one century.
With the division and privatization of Japan National Railways in 1987, JR West took over.
This facility itself is the base of the current train-cars (Umekoji Operation Ward) and is also carried out by steam locomotives and train-cars owned by the Sagano Sightseeing Tram Locomotive Co., Ltd.
In the first place, this facility became the start of the establishment of this facility, and in the latter half of the 1960s, steam locomotives disappeared rapidly during the latter half of the 1960s.
At the beginning, the Koyama Office, which was located near the capital, was the most important candidate for preservation organization in the premises of Oyama Station, Tochigi Prefecture, but it was officially selected for the preservation organization of Umekoji Office (at that time) in 1970 because it was located in the center of Japan, because it was located in the center of the preservation organization.
Basically, the cars for preservation were considered to be selected for the most young train cars (it is possible for the first time to be used for the first time), but there were some cars that were originally scheduled to be preserved in the original form of C62 model No. 22.
The facilities are composed of the former Umekoji Intersection and the "Jocomotive Museum," which utilizes the Sensu and bicycles in the former Umekoji Ward, and the former Nijo Station building was relocated and restored.
The Sensukuri is a reinforced concrete structure constructed in 1914, and it was designated as a national important cultural property along with the hikikomi Line on December 10, 2004.
In 2004, he was selected as a World Chemistry of Chemistry of civil engineering and civil engineering.
In 2006, the former Nijo Station station building (an exhibitment station) and the Sengata-shako Depot were designated as a national monuments by JR West.
The former Nijo Station building is the oldest wooden building in Japan, which was built in 1904 by the head office of the Kyoto Railway Company, and is the oldest wooden building in Japan, and was designed to imitate the Heian-jingu Shrine in consideration of the scenery.
The Kyoto Railway was nationalized in 1907 and was later used as the station house of JR West, but in 1996 it ended as an elevated station station building in the Sanin Main Line (Sagano Line) and Hanazono Station (Kyoto Prefecture) and it was relocated to the main building of the main building in 1996 and used as an important material for the use of old materials.
It was designated as a cultural property in Kyoto City in April 1996.
In Sensukuri, sixteen-yen steam locomotives manufactured from the Taisho period to the Showa period (the first time, seventeen-yen coins were at the beginning of the Edo period) are placed and exhibited.
At the beginning of the opening, the construction was basically preserved, and there were 15 cars of C53/C51 and 2299 cars of C539 cars, but since then, the number of cars for preservation has been carried out several times in 2006 as of 2006 as of the year.
Five cars of five cars are still used for the above-mentioned five cars, and two-car trains (C571/C56/C56) are offered to the trains on the Yamaguchi Line, such as 'Yamagachi (train)' on the Hokuriku Main Line and 'Hokobiko (train)' on the Hokuriku Main Line.
Although there are two types of motorculosis that have been handed down to each other, they are not available at the main line because they are not examined in general.
In addition, the 'SL Locomotive' is operated by the use of an airplanes for displaying the inside of the building.
In the past, this bus service was extended to the north of the station's depot, which is now a large parking lot, but the station building of the former Nijo Station was relocated to its current station building, and it was changed to the south end of the Sagano Line in the present-day Umekoji Park.
Even today, it has its functions as an organization, and it is connected with the commercial line.
In addition, after the abolition of the Takatori factory of West Japan Railway Company (JR West), the steam locomotives were also available.
