Shomyo is one of Japanese traditional music.
It is a religious music used for rituals.
In Japan, it is also called Bonuta or Bonno-ku.
According to the old Chinese characters, it is written as "seimyo" in the old Chinese characters.
It is one of the academic field of ancient India.
Gomyo refers to five kinds of learning (the study of phonology, the study of Calligraphy), the art of crafts, the art of art, the art of medicine, the medicine, the medicine, the Inmei (the study of the ethics), and the Naimyo (the study of Buddhism) (in the case of Buddhism, the study of Buddhism).
It has been handed down to Japan along with the introduction of Buddhism to Japan.
There is a record that a memorial service for the opening of the Great Buddha of Todai-ji Temple was held in 754, and in the Nara period, Shomyo was popular.
In the early Heian period, Saicho and Kukai introduced Shomyo and became the foundation of Tendai Shomyo and Shingon Shomyo.
There is a unique voice of each sect of Buddhism other than the Tendai sect and Shingon sect, and it is still handed down to the present day.
In order to transcribe kuden, there was no Rakufu.
Therefore, it was extremely difficult to learn the teachings.
Later, Hakase was invented.
In addition, there are differences in special terms such as hakase depending on each school.
However, Hakase is a reference to recitation, and if it is intended to learn Shomyo, it is necessary to study kuden (also called Ri) (or junju), and it is not possible to maintain and maintain the school from his master to his disciple.
Therefore, the development of leaders and heirs was essential.
With various conditions, many schools were abolished.
The Tendai Shomyo was based on Saicho's teaching, and its original form was developed.
After Saicho, Ennin and Anzen flourished.
Ryonin, the founder of Yuzu Nenbutsu, is known as the restorer of Yuzu Nenbutsu.
In 1109, Ryonin built Raigo-in Temple (Sakyo Ward, Kyoto City) in Ohara, Kyoto.
The honorific mountain prefix of Raigo-in Temple in Ohara was called 'Gyozan,' which originated in Chinese Shomyo, and was called 'Uo-yama.'
Before long, two temples of Raigo-in Temple and Katsurin-in Temple became known as the dojo of Ohara-ryu Shomyo-ji Temple.
In addition, Jakuhara later became one of the schools, and Ohara had two schools.
Later, Sokai revived Daibara Shomyo.
Tanchi established the foundation based on the new theory of music.
Since then, he had no center of Tendai Shomyo (Buddhist invocation) and succeeded to the current Tendai Shomyo.
The Yuzu Nenbutsu sect, Jodo sect and Jodo Shinshu sect Shomyo are the lineage of Tendai Shomyo.
The Shingon Shomyo is based on the idea that Kukai introduced it to the present day.
The system of Shomyo has been systematized since the era of Shinga.
Among them, Kancho can be said to be the founder of restoration.
He worked for the creation and improvement of shomyo (a Japanese poem of thirty-one syllables).
Until the Kamakura period, many schools had been established by Cloistered Imperial Prince Kakusho, and were organized into four schools (honsoo-in, Shinsoo-in, Daigo-in, and Nakagawa-Oshin-ryu).
The Shomyo of the Kogi Shingon sect declined during the Edo period.
The Honsoo-in school, Shino-in school and Daigo-ryu school were abolished until the middle of the Meiji period.
Today, it is divided into Chizan Shomyo (Kyoto Chishaku-in Temple), Buzan Shomyo (Buzan-ji Temple in Nara) and Nanzan Shinryu School (Nanshan-ryu school, Koyasan, Kyoto and Kogi Shingon sect).
Chizan Shomyo and Buzan Shomyo (Shingi Shingon sect Shomyo) of Shingon sect, Chizan school of Shingon sect, Buzan school of Shingon sect, and Shomyo of both schools originated from the Nakagawa Daijin School.
Raiyu adopted the old school of Daigo.
In 1583, when Negoro-ji Temple (Wakayama Prefecture) was burnt down by Hideyoshi TOYOTOMI, the two schools of Chisan and Toyoyama were established as old schools of Daigo.
The characteristic of the above is 'Rongo' and 'Shomyo' of Mt. Chizan.
The Nanzan Shinryu school (Kogi Shingon sect Shomyo) became the origin of the Nakagawa Daijin School.
The Nakagawa-Oshin-ryu school was founded by Daishin of Nara and Nakagawa-dera Temple.
During the Jocho era (1222-1233), Shoshin of Mt. Koya and Sanboin moved its base to Mt. Koya.
Later, the name of Mt. Koya was prefixed by the name of Mt. Nanzan and was called Nanzan Shinryu.
It is also called Shinryu and Noyama-ryu.
It can be said that the songs such as Heikyoku, Yokyoku, Minyo, and Joruri (dramatic narrative chanted to a shamisen accompaniment) were changed to Shomyo.
It is greatly influenced by a single melody music.
Emperor Tenchi (also known as Emperor Tenchi)
A man.
626 - January 7, 672 (January 7, 672)
He was the thirty-eighth Emperor.
His posthumous name was Amenohohikazuchi no mikoto (also known as Akatsusakawake no mikoto).
His imina (personal name) was Kagura.
It is believed that he was called Katsuragi no Miko (Katsuragi no Miko) before he was born.
It is generally known as Naka no Oe no Oji (Naka no Oe no Oji).
The term 'Oe' refers to the crown prince, and 'Naka no Oe' means 'the next crown prince.'
Summary
He was the second prince of Emperor Jomei.
His mother was Hokyo no himemiko (later Emperor Kogyoku).
His older paternal half-brother was Yamatohime no Okimi, a daughter of his older paternal half-brother, Furuhito no Oe no Oji.
He plotted with NAKATOMI no Kamatari and killed SOGA no Iruka and made his uncle, Emperor Kotoku ascend the throne, and he became the crown prince.
He established the era name of the Taika era and carried out various reforms (the central figure of the Taika Reforms and the Isshi Incident).
In addition, after the coup d'etat such as Prince Arima, he made a coup d'etat with a trap and was sentenced to death.
Since Baekje was destroyed by Silla in 660 and Silla, he sent King of Baekje, who had stayed in the Imperial Court, and tried to restore Baekje.
He stayed in Chikushi in order to save Baekje, but died in 661.
After that, he did not have a title for a long time, but after the defeat in the Battle of Hakusukinoe in 663, he transferred the capital to Otsu and ascended to the throne.
After the Battle of Hakusukinoe, it was established as part of the defense policy of defense of land and defense.
In addition, the official rank system was also carried out in the twenty-sixth grades of cap rank from the previous 19th to the 16th grades of cap rank.
In 670, he created the oldest nationwide family register 'Kogo no Nenjaku' (the year of the Koshin era).
According to Nihonshoki (Chronicles of Japan), Emperor Tenchi had his first son, Otomo no Oji, succeed to the throne.
However, in the Jinshin War which occurred after the death of Emperor Tenchi, his younger brother, Prince Oama (Emperor Tenmu), won the throne and succeeded to the throne.
Since then, the Emperor Tenmu line of the Tenmu line continued until Emperor Shotoku.
After the death of Shotoku, the grandchild of Emperor Tenchi, Shirakabe no Okimi, was enthroned as Emperor Konin, and after that, it became the line of Emperor Tenchi.
It is said that he sent four daughters to the Prince Oama as a wife of Prince Oama because he took the Prince Nukata.
Brief Personal History
626 - He was born.
June 12, 645: He became Crown Prince.
Saimei July 24, 661 - Shosei
Tenchi was enthroned on February 20, 668.
On January 7, 672, he died at the age of 46.
(In "Fuso Ryakki" (A Brief History of Japan), it is said that he was killed by the Emperor Tenmu'
It was one of the mystery of political history in the mid seventh century that Emperor Tenchi did not ascend the throne for a long time.
There are several opinions about this.
It is important to support the forces of the Emperor Tenmu.
In other words, it is wrong to say that Emperor Tenmu was the younger brother of Emperor Tenchi, and it is based on the theory that he was a younger brother of Emperor Tenchi, and he was born before Empress Kogyoku married Emperor Jomei, and he was a half-brother of Emperor Tenchi.
It is certain that, based on the death of Emperor Tenchi in "Nihonshoki" (Chronicles of Japan) and some historical books, the year of Emperor Tenmu's death was changed to Toshicho.
However, there is no contradiction among the same historical materials, and there are many historical materials that set the age difference between 8 and 9.
Against this, there is a theory that the Emperor Jomei's age at the age of 16 in the 'Nihonshoki' (Chronicles of Japan) described in the "Nihonshoki" (Chronicles of Japan) that the Emperor Jomei was enthroned when his father, Emperor Jomei, died.
Therefore, in 614, the actual year of birth was adopted by the Imperial Family, such as "Honcho koin jounroku" (the Emperor's family tree) and so on.
"The Emperor Tenchi and Empress Jito, whose reign was not rare in ancient times, were also called "the Emperor Tenchi" and did not ascend to the throne immediately after the reign of Emperor Tenmu.
Therefore, there was a misfortune.
There is also a theory that it is not treated as the same as that of "Nihonshoki" (Chronicles of Japan) because it is pointed out that "Nihonshoki" (Chronicles of Japan) and other historical books were compiled in the period of the compilation of "Nihonshoki" (Chronicles of Japan).
(Refer to the section of 'Emperor Tenmu's age'.)
There is a theory that the Isshi no Hen was a coup d'etat of Prince Karu (Emperor Kotoku) and Naka no Oe no Oji was expelled from the position.
In recent years, the relationship between Naka no Oe no Oji and SOGA no Iruka is relatively good, and it is pointed out that the basic policy was similar to that of Naka no Oe no Oji.
Then, Naka no Oe no Oji has no motive to kill the deer.
As the description of the Taika Reforms in Nihonshoki (Chronicles of Japan) has been recognized, this theory began to be advocated.
In this theory, the Soga clan, who was not expelled from the position of Empress Kogyoku, and the fact that the Soga clan other than Iruka are not expelled even after the coup d'etat, can be described.
There is a theory that the enthronement of the Emperor was delayed due to the opposition to the relationship between the women of Tenchi.
This is because Emperor Kotoku, who was described in "Nihonshoki" (Chronicles of Japan), wrote a poem to his wife, a daughter of Emperor Tenchi (a younger maternal half-sister of the Emperor Tenchi), suggests the unexpected relation between her and Tenchi.
