Yoshinobu TOKUGAWA (also known as Yoshihisa TOKUGAWA) was the fifteenth seii taishogun (literally,"great general who subdues the barbarians") of the Edo bakufu (Japanese feudal government headed by a shogun) (reigned from December 5, 1866 to December 9, 1867).
He was the only Shogun who did not perform the duties of the shogun in Edo-jo Castle.
He was promoted to Naidaijin (minister of the center) Juichii (Junior First Rank) and the Grand Cordon of the Order of the Merit.
He was a member of Kizokuin (the House of Peers).
After the death of the 14th Shogun Iemochi TOKUGAWA, who served as a guardian of the shogun, he became the fifteenth Shogun of the Edo bakufu (Japanese feudal government headed by a shogun).
After the Emperor Meiji regained power (the Taisei Hokan (transfer of power back to the Emperor), he tried to overthrow the shogunate, but in the Battle of Toba and Fushimi after the Restoration of Imperial Rule (Japan), he returned to Edo-jo Castle after the restoration of imperial rule, and was ordered by the Imperial Court to hunt down and kill the former Shogunate forces, and surrendered Edo-jo Castle.
In the Meiji period, he became a priest and lived until the Taisho period as a prince.
Yoshinobu' is also read as 'Yoshinobu' or 'Keiki.'
In official documents of the Edo bakufu (Japanese feudal government headed by a shogun), there is a record that 'Yoshihisa' was read as 'Yoshihisa' during the official documents of the Edo bakufu (Japanese feudal government headed by a shogun).
