Nara Line is a railway line of West Japan Railway Company (JR West) that runs from Kizu Station (Kyoto Prefecture) in Kizu City, Kyoto Prefecture to Kyoto Station in Shimogyo Ward, Kyoto City, Kyoto Prefecture.
All lines are included in major suburbs.
As the Kansai Main Line has its history along the Kansai Main Line, the official starting point is Kizu Station, but the train runs from Kyoto to Kizu (the number of train numbers is the same) and the opposite (the number of trains is the same).
There is no specific record of the route date, except for the list of lines, and it is described in the section from Kyoto to Kizu.
The color of the line is green, and it is said that the design was selected as 'the image of a cherry blossoms connecting the two ancient capital of Japan.'
Operated by the West Japan Railway Company (railway operator)
Railway distance (operation kilometers): 34.7 km
Track gauge: 1067 mm
Number of stations: 19 (including starting stations)
Double-track sections: between Shinden Station and Uji Station, JR Fujimori Station and Kyoto Station
Electrified sections: The entire line is electrified (the straight line is 1500V).
Block (railway) system: Automatic block system (track section), automatic block system (one-track section)
Block devices: Automatic Train Stop system
Operation regulations for operation: Shin-Osaka Comprehensive Code
Maximum speed: 110 km/h (a double-track section), 95 km/h (a single track)
except Kizu Station, the station is under the control of the Kyoto branch of West Japan Railway Company (JR West Osaka Branch Office).
The line between Kyoto and Kizu is named the Nara Line, but the entire line is located in Kyoto Prefecture, and there is no line in Nara Prefecture.
This is due to the fact that the line between Kyoto and Nara was originally built by Nara Railway, but after the merger with Kansai Railway Company, it was separated into a part of the Nagoya route in Osaka and Nagoya.
However, the train runs from Kizu Station to Nara Station of the Kansai Main Line (Yamatoji Line) to Nara Station in Nara Prefecture.
On March 13, 1988, JR West established the name of the line (including the JR Kyoto Line and JR Kobe Line) with the amendment of the Urban Network, but the Nara Line was sent to the Nara Line.
The Kintetsu Nara Line is also introduced as 'JR Nara Line' because the Kintetsu Nara Line is also operated by Kintetsu Corporation.
In the time of Japan National Railways, it was described as 'Japan National Railways Nara Line,' and the English signboard signboard was also used as 'JNR.'
The Kintetsu Nara Line is related to the Yamatoji Line between Naniwa and Nara, and the Kintetsu Kyoto Line is the Kintetsu Kyoto Line that has a rival relation with the JR Nara Line.
While the Keihan Uji Line and Kintetsu Kyoto Line were operated parallel to the line, the Nara Line was not electrified until 1984 at the end of the JNR era, and even after the electrification of the JNR/JR Suburban Train Series 103 was electrified, only a small rolling stock operated by the JNR/JR Suburban Train Series 103 was used for a while until the privatization of Japan National Railways.
However, after the establishment of JR West, the line was in the Urban Network network, the improvement of transportation rapidly increased, such as four-car trains (a part of the train is six cars), and the number of rapid trains, such as 'Miyakoji Rapid,' and so on.
Today, it has a strong character as a commuter line from the southern part of Kyoto Prefecture, such as Byodo-in Temple in Uji City, which is located along the line, as well as in Joyo City, Kyoto Prefecture.
However, the section between Momoyama Station and Shinden Station is a big detour on the east side, and in the south of Joyo, there is a difference between the Kintetsu Kyoto Line, which runs outside the central part of the town, and the Kintetsu Kyoto Line, which runs parallel to the vicinity of the center of the town.
The train is used for Nara Train Depot of Osaka Branch, but the station facilities are managed by Kyoto Branch except for Kizu Station.
