Higashi Hongan-ji Temple is a Buddhist temple belonging to Jodo Shinshu (the True Pure Land Sect of Buddhism) in Shimogyo Ward, Kyoto City, Kyoto Prefecture.
It is the head temple of the Shinshu sect Otani school (Shinshu Jishu sect), one of the Shinshu sect Otani school, and it is the head temple of Jodo Shinshu sect (the True Pure Land Sect of Buddhism) along with Nishi Hongan-ji Temple (the head temple of the Hongan-ji school of Jodo Shinshu sect) (the True Pure Land Sect of Buddhism).
The formal name of the temple was 'Hongan-ji Temple' until 1987, and after the same year, it was 'Shinshu Honbyo' (Shinshu Honbyo).
It is often called 'Oto-san' to distinguish it from Nishi Hongan-ji Temple in Shimogyo Ward.
(For details, see the section on the history of Hongan-ji Temple.)
When Kyonyo, the twelfth head priest of Hongan-ji Temple, was given the territory in the east of Hongan-ji Temple by Ieyasu TOKUGAWA, and Hongan-ji Temple was established in 1602 when Hongan-ji Temple was divided into two.
It is now located at Karasuma Shichijo, Shimogyo Ward, Kyoto City, and it is now called Higashi Hongan-ji Temple because it is located in the east of Nishi Hongan-ji Temple located in Shichijo, Horikawa-Shichijo.
In 1987, Hongan-ji Temple as a religious corporation, which was related to the Otani school and the Bekoku, was dissolved, and it was considered to be one of the religious schools.
After that, the formal name of Higashi Hongan-ji Temple was 'Shinshu Honbyo' (the Honbyo) (the main mausoleum of the Shinshu sect) (the main mausoleum of the Jodo Shinshu sect) was the Konpon dojo (the main seminary) where believers of the same Shinshu sect (the True Pure Land Sect of Buddhism) listen to the teachings of Shinran, the founder of the sect, Shinran.
It also means to distinguish it from the Higashi Hongan-ji school of Jodo Shinshu (the True Pure Land Sect of Buddhism), which is a branch of the Jodo Shinshu sect.
The Otodo incident (to the east of the castle)
In the Edo period, many of the existing buildings were rebuilt during the Meiji period, although many of the existing buildings were rebuilt during the Meiji period after the fire.
The Miei-do Hall, which houses the statue of Shinran Shonin, is the largest wooden building in the world (now covered with the protective roof).
The Goei-do Gate is one of the three major gates of the Sanmon gate.
It is owned by Shinran's "Ken Jodo Shinshu Jishu Jishu Shomon" (National Treasure) (Bunkyo-bon, Bando-bon) (The Bando-bon manuscript), which is a national treasure of Jodo Shinshu (the True Pure Land Sect of Buddhism).
Shoseien Garden in the detached precinct near the ground is a national scenic spot.
The current head priest is Choken OTANI (Jonyo).
