Amaterasu Omikami (the Sun Goddess) is a god (Shinto) appearing in Japanese Mythology.
In the "Engishiki" (an ancient book for codes and procedures on national rites and prayers), 'Amaterasu' (the sun goddess) is referred to as 'Amida' in the case of natural gods and so on.
It is a god who deified the sun, and is said to be one of the ancestor gods of the Imperial Household.
In "Kojiki" (The Records of Ancient Matters), Amaterasu Omikami is described as Amaterasu Omikami (the Sun Goddess) and in "Nihonshoki" (Chronicles of Japan).
It is also known as Dainichi-no-kami.
In "Nihonshoki" (Chronicles of Japan), the names of several gods are described, while in "Kojiki" (The Records of Ancient Matters), it is unified as 'Amaterasu Omikami' (the Sun Goddess).
Although she is a goddess, there is a different theory that she is actually a male god.
As the name of Amaterasu Omikami (the Sun Goddess) is used in political part such as "Kotai-jingu Shrine Gishiki Cho" (Codes and procedures on national rites and prayers), it is said that Takamimusubi (Takamimusubi no mikoto) was originally the ancestor of the Imperial Family and became Amaterasu Omikami (the Sun Goddess).
In Ise-jingu Shrine, it is usually referred to as the Amaterasu Omikami (the Sun Goddess) or Amaterasu Omikami (the Sun Goddess), and when chanting the name of a deity, it is called Amaterasu Omikami, it is also referred to as Amamatarashi-no-kami.
At present, 'Amida' and 'Katakana' are used in academic documents.
There is a theory that it was written as Amaterasu Omikami (the Sun Goddess) in ancient times.
Description in the mythology
According to the "Kojiki" (The Records of Ancient Matters), he was born from the Yominokuni (the land of the dead) where Izanami lived, and when he washed the impurities of the dead, he was washed ashore when he washed his left eye.
At this time, he was called Sanshi together with Tsukuyomi, who was born from the right eye, and Susanoo who was born from the nose.
At this time, Izanagi ordered Amaterasu Omikami to govern Takamanohara.
Susanoo, who had been delegated to the sea, continued to go to the None Province where Izanami lived, so he was exiled by Izanagi.
Susanoo went to Takamanohara (plain of high heaven) where his older sister Amaterasu Omikami (the Sun Goddess) would meet her older sister Amaterasu Omikami (the Sun Goddess), but Amaterasu Omikami thought that he had come to usurp Takamanohara (plain of high heaven) before he came to Japan.
In order to prove Susanoo's purity, he was born to Amaterasu Omikami (the Sun Goddess) in order to prove the pledge, and the three goddesses were born from Susanoo no kami (the god of five pillars) and Susanoo no kami (the god of three pillars), and Susanoo declared a victory.
The following five gods were born from the real existence of Amaterasu Omikami (the Sun Goddess) and believed to be the child of Amaterasu Omikami (the Sun Goddess).
