Shrines where Amaterasu Omikami (the Sun Goddess) is enshrined
The shrine that enshrines Amaterasu Omikami is called Shinmei-jinja Shrine in various places, but the head shrine is the Naiku (Imperial Shrine) of Ise-jingu Shrine (Ise-jingu Shrine).
The Kotai-jingu Shrine is a Shinto shrine that houses Yata no Kagami (Yata no Kagami) which is one of the three sacred treasures of the Three Sacred Treasures of the Imperial Family.
Most of the shrines throughout Japan are distributed to the Shinto priests of Kotai-jingu Shrine (Amaterasu-o-daijingu Shrine).
The term "daimyo" refers to a term used by daimyo (Japanese territorial lord) who were transferred from daimyo (Japanese territorial lord).
It refers to the most important person.
It is a small name.
Originally, he said that the people who had the power in the local regions had come to Japan.
In the samurai society, the term 'bushi' came to mean samurai who owned many shoryo (territory) and subordinates.
In the Muromachi period, Shugo (provincial constable) strengthened the control of the territory and became shugo daimyo (shugo, which were Japanese provincial military governors, that became daimyo, which were Japanese feudal lords).
In the Sengoku period (period of warring states), a daimyo (Japanese territorial lord in the Sengoku period) called daimyo (Japanese territorial lord in the Sengoku period) or Sengoku daimyo (Japanese territorial lord in the Sengoku period).
In the Edo period, the term came to refer to samurai who were given a fief of more than 10,000 koku of rice by the Edo bakufu (Japanese feudal government headed by a shogun).
A samurai under the direct control of the bakufu (Japanese feudal government headed by a shogun) who was less than 10,000 koku was called jisan.
As daimyo (Japanese territorial lord) had a character of the feudal lord and character of the feudal lord in China, they were also called daimyo lords in accordance with the feudal lords.
