Daimyo (Japanese territorial lord) in the Edo period
In the Edo period, the rank of the daimyo was determined by the social status, official rank, official rank, official rank, and position.
First, it is classified into the gokenin (an immediate vassal of the shogunate in the Kamakura and Muromachi through Edo periods) of the Tokugawa family, as well as the fudai daimyo (a daimyo in hereditary vassal to the Tokugawa family) who was a vassal of the Tokugawa family before the Battle of Sekigahara, as well as to be a vassal of the Tokugawa family.
When the first Shogun family was discontinued, the Tokugawa gosanke (three privileged branches of the Tokugawa family) established the Tokugawa gosanke (three privileged branches of Tokugawa family) which was allowed to use the family name of the Tokugawa family as well as to support the Tokugawa family, and to maintain the blood line of the shogun family, and to keep the Tokugawa family in the whole country, and to support the Tokugawa family, Yoshinobu TOKUGAWA, the tenth son of Yoshinobu TOKUGAWA, the tenth son of the Kishu Domain, Yorifusa TOKUGAWA, the eleventh son of the Kishu Domain.
